# Drive one axis to one end and leave it standing on the switch.
#
# A control loop, not a command: move a chunk, read that end's sensor, stop the
# moment it reads triggered. It is an action because of what it is protecting.
# There are LCD panels mounted around this rig and an axis driven past its limit
# destroys them. The controller's firmware limit stop is the real protection; this
# loop is the software backstop, and a backstop that depends on a network link
# staying up is not one -- so the loop runs on the server, beside the serial port.
#
# Z additionally cannot be crossed in a single command: travel is ~530 mm and the
# protocol caps a linear move at 487.5 mm, so the drive issues repeated legs.
# Do not "simplify" that back into one move.

# "x" | "y" | "z" | "yaw" | "pitch"
string axis

# true  -> the high side (right / up / forward)
# false -> the low side  (left / down / back)
bool high_side

# "low" | "mid" | "high"
string speed
---
bool success

# True when the run ended on the sensor rather than on the travel limit or a
# timeout. False here with success true means it stopped for a reason that was
# not the switch, which the operator needs to see.
bool reached_sensor

string coordinate
bool cancelled
string message
---
# Sensor tri-state as the loop sees it: 1 triggered, 0 clear, -1 unreadable.
int32 sensor
string coordinate
string stage
